@@2
From: Gordon Kessler
To: All
Subj: Why the 386 Wins
Date: 01-16-89 02:40

Since the sysop has kindly given me a room to shout in, let me say what
I came to say, once, completely, so nobody can ever claim I didn't warn
them: the 386 wins, and it wins for reasons that have nothing to do with
how many registers you can count on your fingers or how pretty your
instruction encoding looks on a poster, because the 386 runs every
program that was ever written for the PC, all of it, the whole
mountain, every spreadsheet and every game and every accounting package
that somebody's uncle wrote in 1983 and nobody has the source code for
any more, and the ARM runs what, exactly, a nice clean new set of
programs that were all written in the last year by people who think
elegance is a feature, when every working person in this country knows
that the only feature is "does it run the thing I already have", and
that's before we even get to the instructions themselves, which on the
386 do REAL WORK, a single REP MOVSB moves a whole block of memory
while your ARM is still loading its sixteen registers one at a time
like a clerk counting change, and don't tell me about "load/store
architecture" as if having to load something before you can add to it
were a virtue instead of an extra step that the 386 simply doesn't make
you take, because on my machine I can add straight to memory, in one
instruction, like an adult, and as for segments, which you people love
to laugh at, segments are protection, segments are organisation,
segments are how a real operating system knows which memory belongs to
whom, and the fact that your "flat" address space lets any program
scribble anywhere it likes is not freedom, it's anarchy, and when the
big operating systems of the 1990s arrive they will run in protected
mode on the 386 and they will use every one of those segment registers
you've been mocking, and your ARM will be sitting in the corner running
a BBS in somebody's spare bedroom, and I say that with respect for the
sysop, who is a fine person with questionable taste in processors, and
while I'm on the subject of taste, let me address the so-called
"pipeline" that you all keep bringing up at Sal's Pizza as if you
invented it, because the 386 has a pipeline too, and a prefetch queue,
and it has had them since before your chip had a name, and the
difference is that ours is hidden inside where it belongs instead of
being printed on T-shirts, and let me also address the TURBO button,
which on your machines goes from 12 to 100 and which you all seem to
think is a personality, when a turbo button is an admission that the
machine was too fast for the software and had to be slowed down on
purpose to be useful, which is not a boast, it is a confession, and
speaking of confessions, I would like to hear one of you confess that
you have ever needed thirty-two bits of address space for anything
other than feeling superior, because 640K is a great deal of memory and
I have run an entire bulletin board in it, with doors, and I have never
once wished for a flat model, not once, not even on the night the
message base hit 400K and the whole Fortress had to be reorganised by
hand, and
finally, and I want this on the record, clock for clock the 386 has
more transistors than your ARM, many more, and more transistors means
more thinking, and more thinking means more work done, and nobody ever
got fired for buying more thinking, and I have run The 386 Fortress on
a 386 since the day the chip came out and it has never once lost a
message to line noise or a train, which is more than some boards I
could mention can say, and that is why the 386 wins, and I will be
happy to accept your apologies at 555-0386, any time, day or night,
the line is always up, because it runs on a 386.

Gordon Kessler, sysop, The 386 Fortress
