From: Susan Oyelaran
To: Gordon Kessler
Subj: Re: Why the 386 Wins
Date: 01-16-89 07:55

> more transistors means more thinking

Gordon, I read the whole thing.  It took my whole breakfast.  In return,
three short points, with paragraphs, as a gift:

The ARM has fewer transistors because it doesn't need a microcoded
decoder to untangle instructions of one to fifteen bytes.  Every ARM
instruction is 32 bits.  The transistors it saves go into doing the
work, not figuring out what the work is.

"Loading its sixteen registers one at a time": LDM loads up to sixteen
registers in ONE instruction.  I posted the loop in Programmers' Corner.

And the old programs: that's a fair point.  Genuinely.  It's the best
point in your message.  I'll give you that one, with a bow.

Susan
@@4
From: Karen Whitfield
To: Gordon Kessler
Subj: Re: Why the 386 Wins
Date: 01-17-89 06:30

> on my machine I can add straight to memory, in one instruction

You can write it in one instruction.  The chip still has to read the
memory, add, and write it back, and it takes the cycles to do all
three.  The ARM just makes you write down what it's going to do anyway.

Also, for the record: 16 general registers on the ARM, 8 on the 386,
one of which is the stack pointer.  Every value you keep in a register
is a trip to memory you don't make.

KW
